# LimeRFE, for sdroxide's LimeRFE control over the board's own USB port.
#
# This file is only about the *LimeRFE*, not the LimeSDR. sdroxide reaches the
# LimeSDR through LimeSuite, which opens the USB device itself and ships its own
# rules (64-limesuite.rules, in the limesuite package) — so there is nothing for
# this project to add there, and duplicating it would only make it unclear which
# rule owns those ids.
#
# The LimeRFE is different: sdroxide opens its serial port directly, and on most
# distributions a USB serial device is owned by root and the `dialout` group.
#
# TAG+="uaccess" hands an ACL to whoever is logged in at the active seat. That
# is better than adding the user to `dialout`, which needs a group to exist, the
# user to be added to it, and a full re-login before it takes effect.
#
# Install:
#   sudo cp 60-sdroxide-limerfe.rules /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload
# then unplug and replug the board.
#
# The id below is a *generic FTDI* one, shared with a great many unrelated
# USB-serial adapters — it cannot identify a LimeRFE and this rule does not try
# to. It grants access; the operator picks the port in Settings → Radio; and the
# board's own hello handshake is what actually confirms a LimeRFE is on the
# other end of it. The consequence is that this rule also loosens permissions on
# any other FTDI serial adapter on the machine, which is the same trade every
# distribution's own FTDI rules already make.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0403", ATTRS{idProduct}=="6001", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"

# Some LimeRFE units carry the FT230X's own id instead.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="0403", ATTRS{idProduct}=="6015", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"
